# Line endings and text attributes, settled before the first data file lands
# (#29).
#
# The default. Every path git detects as text is normalised to a line feed on
# the way into the index and checked out with a line feed, whatever a clone's
# core.autocrlf happens to be. This project is written on Windows, where a
# checkout that carries carriage returns into a tracked text file makes the hash
# of a regenerated case differ from the hash in the index. That reads as a
# determinism failure and is not one.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Text by name rather than by detection, so a file whose first bytes are unusual
# is not guessed at.
*.rs           text eol=lf
*.toml         text eol=lf
*.md           text eol=lf
*.yml          text eol=lf
*.yaml         text eol=lf
*.txt          text eol=lf
*.json         text eol=lf
.gitattributes text eol=lf
.gitignore     text eol=lf
Cargo.lock     text eol=lf

# Binary, never touched. `binary` is git's own macro for `-text -diff`, so
# nothing converts a line ending in one of these and a diff of one is a diff of
# bytes.
*.png  binary
*.pdf  binary
*.npy  binary
*.bin  binary
*.gz   binary
*.zip  binary

# The trace file is classed by the two lines above rather than by a line of its
# own. Its array is an `.npy`, which is binary and never converted, and its
# header is a `.json`, which is text with a line feed and carries no carriage
# return to lose. That is #35, and the format is `docs/format/trace.md`.
#
# The truth file and the case archive have no class here yet, because their
# formats are not written. Whichever of #36 and #37 lands first declares its own
# class in this file, and a data format that arrives without one is covered by
# the default above, which is a guess.
#
# A fixture whose bytes are the point is not covered by any line in this file,
# and cannot be. Attributes decide what happens to a file on the way into git,
# and a fixture that exists to prove a reader handles a carriage return would be
# normalised by the default above and silently lose the byte it was created for.
# So the rule for those is that they are held encoded in source rather than as
# raw literals in a tracked file, and what a test feeds a reader is what the
# test wrote, whatever any attribute here says.
